  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both ACE inhibitors (angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors) and ARBs (angiotensin receptor blockers) are recognized as effective first-line treatments for essential hypertension.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Both ACE inhibitors (angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors) and ARBs (angiotensin receptor blockers) are recognized as effective first-line treatments for essential hypertension"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "How They Work"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "ACE Inhibitors: Block the enzyme that produces angiotensin II, a substance that narrows blood vessels. Examples include lisinopril, ramipril, and enalapril.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "ARBs: Block the specific receptors that angiotensin II binds to, preventing blood vessel constriction. Examples include losartan and valsartan.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              4
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Differences as First-Line Options"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Efficacy: Both classes are equally effective at lowering systolic blood pressure (SBP), with ACE inhibitors offering a minor or comparable effect on diastolic blood pressure (DBP).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effects (Cough): ACE inhibitors frequently cause a persistent, dry, nagging cough in a minority of patients due to the buildup of bradykinin. ARBs do not block bradykinin breakdown in the same way, making a dry cough rare.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Tolerability: Because of the lower rate of side effects like cough, ARBs are often preferred or chosen over ACE inhibitors when starting new therapy. If a patient cannot tolerate an ACE inhibitor, an ARB is a standard direct substitute.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Combination Warning: Do not combine an ACE inhibitor and an ARB together. Using both simultaneously does not provide better outcomes and significantly increases the risk of side effects like high potassium (hyperkalemia) and kidney dysfunction .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6,
              7
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Are you comparing these for yourself or someone else ?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Are there other health conditions (such as diabetes, kidney disease, or heart failure) or side effects to consider?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help you look at how these factors influence treatment choices."
      }
